.TH SSH 2
.SH NAME
ssh: connect, login, run, Conn, writepkt, readpkt \- SSH-2 client
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "sys.m";
include "keyring.m";
include "security.m";
include "encoding.m";
include "asn1.m";
include "pkcs.m";
include "ssh.m";
ssh := load Ssh Ssh->PATH;

init:       fn();
connect:    fn(addr, host, wantthumb: string): (ref Conn, string);
rsakey_pem: fn(pem: string): (ref Authkey, string);
login:      fn(c: ref Conn, user: string, key: ref Authkey,
                password: string): string;
run:        fn(c: ref Conn, cmd: string): (ref Result, string);

Conn: adt {
        fd:        ref Sys->FD;
        vc:        string;
        vs:        string;
        encrypted: int;
        sid:       array of byte;
        hostkey:   array of byte;
        thumb:     string;

        readpkt:  fn(c: self ref Conn): (array of byte, string);
        writepkt: fn(c: self ref Conn, payload: array of byte): string;
        close:    fn(c: self ref Conn);
};
Authkey: adt { blob: array of byte; n, d: ref IPint; modlen: int; };
Result:  adt { stdout, stderr: array of byte; status: int; };

MSG_KEXINIT, MSG_NEWKEYS, MSG_SERVICE_REQUEST,
MSG_SERVICE_ACCEPT, MSG_USERAUTH_REQUEST, ...: con ...;
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Ssh
is an SSH-2 transport-and-connection client written in Limbo, ported
from 9front's
.I /sys/src/cmd/ssh.c
and built on this tree's own cryptography \(em no host
.IR ssh (1),
no
.B #C
transport. It speaks exactly one of everything the modern OpenSSH
default settled on, the same deliberately small surface
.I ssh.c
carries: the
.B curve25519-sha256
key exchange, an
.B ssh-rsa
host key whose exchange-hash signature is
.BR rsa-sha2-256 ,
and the
.B chacha20-poly1305@openssh.com
authenticated packet cipher. There is no agent, no GSSAPI, and no
compression.
.PP
.B Init
must be called once before anything else; it loads the
supporting modules
.RI ( keyring (2),
.IR pkcs (2)
for the RSA host-key verification, the
.B Random
and
.B Encoding
modules, and the raw ChaCha20 / one-shot Poly1305 keyring primitives
\(em see
.IR chachatest (1)).
.PP
.B Connect
dials
.I addr
(a real dial string such as
.BR tcp!140.82.113.3!22 ,
since dial-by-name is not wired up under hosted
.IR emu ),
runs the SSH version exchange and key exchange against
.IR host ,
and verifies the server's
.B rsa-sha2-256
signature over the exchange hash. It returns only if that signature
checks out, so a returned
.B Conn
has a cryptographically authenticated transcript. On success the
connection is encrypted and the session keys are installed; the caller
then drives userauth and channels over
.B writepkt
and
.BR readpkt .
.PP
.I Host
is used in the exchange hash and in messages.
.I Wantthumb ,
if non-empty, must equal the server's computed
.B "sha256=<base64>"
host-key thumbprint or the connection is refused: this is a
fail-closed check, with no silent trust-on-first-use. The thumbprint
is the OpenSSH
.B SHA256:
fingerprint form (\c
.IR ssh-keyscan "(1) then " ssh-keygen (1)
on a Unix host yield the same value), and is also left in
.B Conn.thumb
for a caller that wants to implement its own known-hosts policy.
.PP
.B Writepkt
frames, pads, encrypts and sends one packet whose
.I payload
begins with the SSH message-type byte.
.B Readpkt
reads one packet, verifies its Poly1305 tag
.I before
decrypting the body, and returns the decrypted payload (message-type
byte first) or an error at end of file or on a bad tag. Both honour
.B Conn.encrypted:
the version exchange and key exchange run in the clear, and the
ciphers switch on together at
.BR MSG_NEWKEYS .
.PP
.B Close
drops the connection.
.SS Authentication and commands
.B Rsakey_pem
parses a PEM PKCS#1 RSA private key \(em the format
.B "ssh-keygen -m PEM"
writes \(em into an
.B Authkey:
the
.B ssh-rsa
public-key blob to present (byte-identical to an
.B authorized_keys
line's base64 body) and the private
.RB ( n , d )
used to sign.
.PP
.B Login
authenticates to a connected server as
.IR user .
It sends the
.B ssh-userauth
service request, then tries publickey (when
.I key
is non-nil) and password (when
.I password
is non-empty), in that order, returning
.B nil
once the server accepts. The publickey exchange sends the unsigned
probe first, and on
.B PK_OK
signs the session id and request with RSASSA-PKCS1-v1_5 over SHA-256
(computed directly \(em
.IR pkcs (2)'s
.B rsa_sign
predates rsa-sha2-256 and is not used).
.PP
.B Run
opens a session channel, execs
.IR cmd ,
and reads to the end of the channel, returning a
.B Result
whose
.B stdout
and
.B stderr
hold the two data streams and whose
.B status
is the remote exit status.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/ssh.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR sshtest (1),
.IR chachatest (1),
.IR tls12 (2),
.IR pkcs (2),
.IR keyring (2)
.SH BUGS
Only one algorithm of each kind is offered; a server without
.B curve25519-sha256
or
.B chacha20-poly1305@openssh.com
cannot be reached, by design.
.PP
.B Run
is one-shot and non-interactive: it advertises a large receive window
and never sends channel data, so it suits commands whose output is
bounded, not an interactive shell (which needs a pty and the two-way
data pump, driven over
.B writepkt
/
.BR readpkt ,
and is not packaged here). Key re-exchange during a long session is
not implemented. Only file-based RSA keys are handled;
.IR factotum (4)
integration is separate.
